      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To determine how many students did not receive an \"A\" in either history or math, we can use the principle of inclusion and exclusion. Let's define the following:\n\n- Let \\( H \\) be the set of students who received an \"A\" in history.\n- Let \\( M \\) be the set of students who received an \"A\" in math.\n\nWe are given the following information:\n- The total number of students is 30.\n- The number of students who received an \"A\" in history, \\( |H| \\), is 7.\n- The number of students who received an \"A\" in math, \\( |M| \\), is 13.\n- The number of students who received an \"A\" in both history and math, \\( |H \\cap M| \\), is 4.\n\nWe need to find the number of students who received an \"A\" in either history or math or both, which is \\( |H \\cup M| \\). According to the principle of inclusion and exclusion, we have:\n\n\\[\n|H \\cup M| = |H| + |M| - |H \\cap M|\n\\]\n\nSubstituting the given values, we get:\n\n\\[\n|H \\cup M| = 7 + 13 - 4 = 16\n\\]\n\nThis means that 16 students received an \"A\" in either history or math or both. To find the number of students who did not receive an \"A\" in either course, we subtract the number of students who received an \"A\" in either course from the total number of students:\n\n\\[\n30 - |H \\cup M| = 30 - 16 = 14\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the number of students who did not receive an \"A\" in any of these two courses is \\(\\boxed{14}\\).",
